ler approach is to recognize that the 38 students who suggested adding bacon are already included in the 450 students who suggested adding mashed potatoes. Therefore, the total number of students who participated in the suggestion of new food items is simply 450.\n\nMy own solving process step by step:\n\n1. Recognize that the 38 students who suggested adding bacon are already included in the 450 students who suggested adding mashed potatoes.\n2. Therefore, the total number of students who participated in the suggestion of new food items is simply 450.\n\nThe answer is 450"], "Math SolverqwBd": ["Let's think step by step.\n\nWe know that 38 students suggested adding bacon to the menu, and the rest did not participate. Since 450 students suggested adding mashed potatoes, we can assume that all 450 students participated in the suggestion of new food items, including those who suggested adding bacon.\n\nThe total number of students who participated is 450.\n\nThe answer is 450"]}], "Response": ["450"], "Attempt answer": "450", "Solved": false, "Total solved": 21, "Total executed": 27, "Accuracy": 0.7777777777777778}
##########Final Log:{"Question": {"task": "Matthew gave equal numbers of crackers and cakes to his 28 friends. If he had 13 crackers and 15 cakes initially How many crackers and cakes did each person eat?", "step": "", "answer": 1.0}, "Answer": 1.0, "Step": "", "All_answers": [{"Math SolverUHMC": ["Q: Angelo and Melanie want to plan how many hours over the next week they should study together for their test next week.\n\nLet's think step by step.\n\nAngelo and Melanie think they should dedicate 3 hours to each of the 2 chapters, 3 hours x 2 chapters = 6 hours total.\nFor the worksheets they plan to dedicate 1.5 hours for each worksheet, 1.5 hours x 4 worksheets = 6 hours total.\nAngelo and Melanie need to start with planning 12 hours to study, at 4 hours a day, 12 / 4 = 3 days.\n\nHowever, they need to include time for breaks and lunch. Every hour they want to include a 10-minute break, \nso 12 total hours x 10 minutes = 120 extra minutes for breaks.\nThey also want to include 3 10-minute snack breaks, 3 x 10 minutes = 30 minutes.\nAnd they want to include 30 minutes for lunch each day, so 120 minutes for breaks + 30 minutes for snack breaks + 30 minutes for lunch = 180 minutes, or 180 / 60 minutes per hour = 3 extra hours.\n\nSo Angelo and Melanie want to plan 12 hours to study + 3 hours of breaks = 15 hours total.\nThey want to study no more than 4 hours each day, 15 hours / 4 hours each day = 3.75\nThey will need to plan to study 4 days to allow for all the time they need.\n\nThe answer is 4"], "Mathematical AnalystLTec": ["Q: There are 15 trees in the grove. Grove workers will plant trees in the grove today. After they are done, there will be 21 trees. How many trees did the grove workers plant today?\n\n## Problem solving process analysis\n\nThere are {ori_tree_num} trees originally.\nThen there were {after_planted_tree_num} trees after some more were planted.\nSo the number of trees planted today {today_planted_num} is the number of trees after planting {after_planted_tree_num} minus the number of trees before planting {ori_tree_num}.\nThe answer is {today_planted_num} = {after_planted_tree_num} - {ori_tree_num}.\n\n## Actual analysis and solution process\n\nIn this question, {ori_tree_num} = 15 and {after_planted_tree_num} = 21.\nThere are 15 trees originally. \nThen there were 21 trees after some more were planted. \nSo the number of trees planted today must have been 21 - 15 = 6.\nThe answer is 6\n\nQ: Leah had 32 chocolates and her sister had 42. If they ate 35, how many pieces do they have left in total?\n\n## Problem solving process analysis\n\nOriginally, Leah had {Leah_num} Leah_num chocolates.\nHer sister had {sister_num} chocolates.\nSo in total they had {all_num} = {Leah_num} + {sister_num} chocolates.\nAfter eating {eating_num} chocolates, the number of chocolates they have left {remain_num} is {all_num} minus {eating_num}. \nThe answer is {remain_num} = {all_num} - {eating_num}.\n\n## Actual analysis and solution process\n\nIn this question, {Leah_num} = 32, {sister_num} = 42 and {eating_num} = 35.\nSo, in total they had 32 + 42 = 74 chocolates originally.\nAfter eating 35 chocolates, they had 74 - 35 = 39 chocolates.\nThe answer is 39\n\nQ: Matthew gave equal numbers of crackers and cakes to his 28 friends. If he had 13 crackers and 15 cakes initially, how many crackers and cakes did each person eat?\n\n## Problem solving process analysis\n\nMatthew had {cracker_num} crackers and {cake_num} cakes initially.\nHe gave equal numbers of crackers and cakes to his {friends_num} friends.\nSo each person got {cracker_per_person} = {cracker_num} / {friends_num} crackers and {cake_per_person} = {cake_num} / {friends_num} cakes.\nThe answer is {cracker_per_person} and {cake_per_person}.\n\n## Actual analysis and solution process\n\nIn this question, {cracker_num} = 13, {cake_num} = 15 and {friends_num} = 28.\nMatthew had 13 crackers and 15 cakes initially.\nHe gave equal numbers of crackers and cakes to his 28 friends.\nSo each person got 13 / 28 = 0.464 (rounded to 3 decimal places) crackers and 15 / 28 = 0.536 (rounded to 3 decimal places) cakes.\nThe answer is 0.464 and 0.536"], "Programming ExpertRMBb": ["Q: Matthew gave equal numbers of crackers and cakes to his 28 friends. If he had 13 crackers and 15 cakes initially, how many crackers and cakes did each person eat?\n\n## Problem solving process analysis\n\nMatthew had {cracker_num} crackers and {cake_num} cakes initially.\nHe gave equal numbers of crackers and cakes to his {friends_num} friends.\nSo each person got {cracker_per_person} = {cracker_num} / {friends_num} crackers and {cake_per_person} = {cake_num} / {friends_num} cakes.\nThe answer is {cracker_per_person} and {cake_per_person}.\n\n## Actual analysis and solution process\n\nIn this question, {cracker_num} = 13, {cake_num} = 15 and {friends_num} = 28.\nMatthew had 13 crackers and 15 cakes initially.\nHe gave equal numbers of crackers and cakes to his 28 friends.\nSo each person got 13 / 28 = 0.464 (rounded to 3 decimal places) crackers and 15 / 28 = 0.536 (rounded to 3 decimal places) cakes.\nThe answer is 0.464 and 0.536\n\n## Code\n\n```python\ndef crackers_and_cakes():\n    cracker_num = 13\n    cake_num = 15\n    friends_num = 28\n    cracker_per_person = cracker_num / friends_num\n    cake_per_person = cake_num / friends_num\n    return cracker_per_person, cake_per_person\n\ncracker_per_person, cake_per_person = crackers_and_cakes()\nprint(f\"Each person got {cracker_per_person} crackers and {cake_per_person} cakes.\")\n```\nthe answer is None"], "Inspector5BpJ": ["To solve the problem, let's break it down step by step.\n\nMatthew had 13 crackers and 15 cakes initially. He gave equal numbers of crackers and cakes to his 28 friends.\n\nStep 1: Find the total number of crackers and cakes Matthew gave to his friends.\nTotal crackers = 13\nTotal cakes = 15\n\nSince Matthew gave equal numbers of crackers and cakes to his friends, we can divide the total number 